#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237782 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133870 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2bcfcb6-8f8b-5aa9-8346-05db880fa5bb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Polynomials is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "136134d6-c99a-5d92-80be-5b0fc7ed5577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements is true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235928} {:answer/id #uuid "27faffec-9d0f-50c1-acc2-8047b07f62ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236069} {:answer/id #uuid "3954784a-f1a2-5aaa-a859-1582c8d098de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236181} {:answer/id #uuid "cf54ecd4-84e4-511b-914e-a768448e333d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237132} {:answer/id #uuid "ef10307d-c75f-54c2-8d28-65c429dd22ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef10307d-c75f-54c2-8d28-65c429dd22ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237325} :answer-field/id #uuid "489c7c18-7902-540c-b4cd-26c22771d625" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e262e4d-a912-58d1-aa39-b29d3810a43d" :question/math-academy-id "q-100119" :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t-2,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t+2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=2t+4.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-2)q(-2)+p(2)q(2),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-2,t+2⟩ \\\\  & =(t-2){|}_{-2}\\cdot (t+2){|}_{-2}+(t-2){|}_{2}\\cdot (t+2){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(-2-2)\\cdot ((-2)+2)+(2-2)\\cdot (2+2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-2,2t+4⟩ \\\\  & =(t-2){|}_{-2}\\cdot (2t+4){|}_{-2}+(t-2){|}_{2}\\cdot (2t+4){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(-2-2)\\cdot (2(-2)+4)+(2-2)\\cdot (2(2)+4) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t+2,2t+4⟩ \\\\  & =(t+2){|}_{-2}\\cdot (2t+4){|}_{-2}+(t+2){|}_{2}\\cdot (2t+4){|}_{2} \\\\  & =((-2)+2)\\cdot (2(-2)+4)+(2+2)\\cdot (2(2)+4) \\\\  & =32\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133870 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2bcfcb6-8f8b-5aa9-8346-05db880fa5bb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Polynomials is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c83ba197-baf1-5a47-b9ea-ad671205f039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380395} {:answer/id #uuid "460f8ed1-22bf-558b-8f1d-b4d2c002aa5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380396} {:answer/id #uuid "c602afc3-785d-515a-b903-b1afc4adfb10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380397} {:answer/id #uuid "decb6e79-8728-54fc-9a0b-c9dd44e09e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements is true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380398} {:answer/id #uuid "20946bc2-8448-5c07-b5d7-0292baab0c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c602afc3-785d-515a-b903-b1afc4adfb10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380397} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdd30202-889c-59ba-bb4d-be2188ca8246" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb972e63-3e46-543b-9a40-0120a6183656" :question/math-academy-id "q-100120" :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t-4,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=2-t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=1-t.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(1)q(1)+p(2)q(2),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t-4,1-t⟩ \\\\  & =(2t-4){|}_{1}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{1}+(2t-4){|}_{2}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2(1)-4)\\cdot (1-1)+(2(2)-4)\\cdot (1-2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2-t,1-t⟩ \\\\  & =(2-t){|}_{1}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{1}+(2-t){|}_{2}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (1-1)+(2-2)\\cdot (1-2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t-4,2-t⟩ \\\\  & =(2t-4){|}_{1}\\cdot (2-t){|}_{1}+(2t-4){|}_{2}\\cdot (2-t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2(1)-4)\\cdot (2-1)+(2(2)-4)\\cdot (2-2) \\\\  & =-2\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133870 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2bcfcb6-8f8b-5aa9-8346-05db880fa5bb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Polynomials is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51c38ba3-947f-5e44-8a81-230ba3328424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380408} {:answer/id #uuid "a5bcb700-18f0-56e2-9cae-eb30dc7e16d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380409} {:answer/id #uuid "e1048327-e452-5193-9750-011189b52b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380410} {:answer/id #uuid "d056adf6-c365-548e-bc8e-b0eccd6d5134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380411} {:answer/id #uuid "06234a79-3f8c-59b1-87c1-abec77e9f5a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1048327-e452-5193-9750-011189b52b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380410} :answer-field/id #uuid "f53b0d47-d591-5204-a1db-1c099fe1fb90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39aa7418-1a3f-5a9e-9642-6c49cf6dc36a" :question/math-academy-id "q-100129" :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t+2,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t-2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=t+1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(2)q(2),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t+2,t-2⟩ \\\\  & =(2t+2){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t-2){|}_{-1}+(2t+2){|}_{2}\\cdot (t-2){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2(-1)+2)\\cdot ((-1)-2)+(2(2)+2)\\cdot (2-2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-2,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(t-2){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1}+(t-2){|}_{2}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{2} \\\\  & =((-1)-2)\\cdot ((-1)+1)+(2-2)\\cdot (2+1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t+2,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(2t+2){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1}+(2t+2){|}_{2}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2(-1)+2)\\cdot ((-1)+1)+(2(2)+2)\\cdot (2+1) \\\\  & =18\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362391 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cc59c81-637e-52e7-92da-29524f62b7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361322} {:answer/id #uuid "802fb970-ace7-5f4e-97e6-b37c8d31083d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361407} {:answer/id #uuid "899a8db7-6c1c-587d-911a-d033dfff4c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361448} {:answer/id #uuid "a5985011-8eed-525d-a132-fa5c90200b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361579} {:answer/id #uuid "c084fbdf-f594-575b-a303-5e655bf5a067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "802fb970-ace7-5f4e-97e6-b37c8d31083d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361407} :answer-field/id #uuid "45b83442-8c98-555a-a0e1-62d1cef63835" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c3132dd-8104-5475-a04a-6ba3130fe419" :question/math-academy-id "q-99822" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,{f}_{3}(t)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[0,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{1},{f}_{2}⟩=0.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,1⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨{sin}^{2}⁡t,1⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡\\alpha =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\alpha )}{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[t-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}]{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\pi -\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{2}-(0-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\pi -0-(0-0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{2}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{2}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c4a1f08-b0f1-5a1b-acc0-21bb784ad758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360885} {:answer/id #uuid "4bb8d109-8a0a-5cd9-b031-5643137e401f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361165} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec89f85-3d50-5b28-9284-a6ca3b103f85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361329} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa7dda3-d138-5e2f-93cb-6aa10e847b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361379} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c106d9-8717-568d-b8c9-93966eb2cbe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bb8d109-8a0a-5cd9-b031-5643137e401f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361165} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd0740c3-55af-55b0-9072-37fabbd9d3dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30fad2b1-33b1-5011-baca-2c53469ec04d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99827" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=1,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t),$ ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ and ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}]